{"availability":"available","full":"saxenamayank-20/ExpenseFlow","name":"ExpenseFlow","description":"ExpenseFlow is a personal expense tracker built around your salary. Each payday starts a new salary period, whatever is left carries over to the next one, and charts and quick insights show where your money went.","category":"Design","language":"JavaScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-08-27T05:49:39Z","updated":"2026-10-09T06:42:27Z","branch":"main","homepage":"https://expense-flow-opal.vercel.app","topics":["fastapi","neondb","postgresql","python","react-vite","render","tailwindcss","vercel"],"demo":"https://expense-flow-opal.vercel.app/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:10.756Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:10.756Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:10.756Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-12T08:00:10.756Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A small app I built to track my spending one salary at a time.","I get paid once a month, and in a normal expense list every month blurs into one long list. I wanted each salary to be its own thing, so I can see what came in, what went out and what was left before the next one arrived."],"features":["Add a salary every payday. It covers everything you spend from that day until the next salary, so expenses land in the right one by their date (past salaries work too).","Whatever is left from a salary carries over into the next one, and overspending gets taken out of it.","Salary history shows each salary's amount, spent and saved. Click one to see all of its expenses and a category breakdown.","Dashboard: current salary, a date range picker, quick insights (top category, biggest expense, this month vs last), totals, and the Add Expense form.","Analytics: spending by category, daily and monthly charts, and salary vs spent for every salary. Each chart has a short note on what it shows and a takeaway from your data."],"sou